🔐 Step-by-Step Breakdown of What We Did
1️⃣ Generated an Ed25519 Key Pair (Client Side)

On your Git Bash (workstation), you created:

backend1_ed25519        → private key (stays on your machine)
backend1_ed25519.pub    → public key (goes to server)


Why Ed25519?

Modern

Smaller

Faster

More secure than RSA

Recommended by OpenSSH

2️⃣ Placed the Public Key in the Correct User’s Home

This was the critical fix.

SSH works per-user.

When you run:

ssh backend1@192.168.56.71


SSH checks:

/home/backend1/.ssh/authorized_keys


Originally, the key was inside:

/home/vagrant/.ssh/


Which is why login failed.

We corrected it by:

sudo mkdir -p /home/backend1/.ssh
sudo nano /home/backend1/.ssh/authorized_keys


Then pasting backend1’s public key there.

3️⃣ Set Proper Ownership and Permissions (Very Important)

SSH is strict about permissions.

We set:

sudo chown -R backend1:backend1 /home/backend1/.ssh
sudo chmod 700 /home/backend1/.ssh
sudo chmod 600 /home/backend1/.ssh/authorized_keys


Why?

SSH refuses login if:

.ssh is writable by others

authorized_keys has loose permissions

files are owned by the wrong user

This step made SSH trust the key.

4️⃣ Ensured Home Directory Was Correct for a Human User

Because backend1 is a human login user:

/home/backend1 → owned by backend1


This allows:

Writing files

Creating configs

Normal shell usage

We avoided making it root-owned (which would break usability).

5️⃣ Restarted SSH Service
sudo systemctl restart ssh


This ensured:

Updated sshd_config was active

Public key authentication was enabled

6️⃣ Logged In Using the Private Key

From Git Bash:

####      ssh -i ~/.ssh/backend1_ed25519 backend1@192.168.56.71        ######


Here’s what happens behind the scenes:

Client says: “I am backend1.”

Server checks /home/backend1/.ssh/authorized_keys

Server finds matching public key

Server challenges client

Client signs with private key

Server verifies signature

Login granted

No password transmitted.

